+### Source-Led UI Parity and Root-Cause Workstreams
+
+**Author:** David Ortinau (via Copilot)
+**Date:** 2026-09-06
+**Status:** Active directive
+
+**Decision:**
+1. Use the global `source-led-ui-parity` skill for any UI rewrite or parity task
+   that has an existing reference implementation. The process is framework
+   agnostic.
+2. Treat the pinned reference source as the product specification. Use the
+   running reference app and screenshots as supporting evidence.
+3. Complete and review the full screen-state discrepancy inventory before
+   editing implementation code.
+4. Keep atomic visual observations as acceptance evidence, then map them to
+   shared implementation workstreams. Do not implement the same symptom
+   separately on each page.
+5. Fix root ownership first: Safe Area and insets, typography and tokens,
+   shared headers and viewports, fixed action rows, navigation, list templates,
+   editor templates, picker templates, and modal shells.
+6. Read framework, native platform, and third-party component source before
+   replacing or styling a named control. A look-alike is not parity.
+7. Verify every mapped screen and state after a shared fix. Automated visual
+   scores support the review but do not replace human visual acceptance.
+
+**BaristaNotes application:** The 91 observed discrepancies are consolidated
+into 16 dependency-ordered workstreams. Seven observations remain marked for
+revalidation against the latest deployed binary.
+
+**Impact:** Holden owns cross-screen root causes and framework boundaries. Amos
+owns shared controls and source-faithful sample integration. Bobbie owns the
+screen-state acceptance matrix and regression evidence.
+
+---
+
 ## Governance
 
 - All meaningful changes require team consensus
